      "math_academy_id": "q-278361",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "From **top to bottom,** the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1$ are\n\n$($\n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}} $)\\,$\nand\n$\\,($\n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}} $).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$",
